 // manifestTimeout bounds the fetch. Home Assistant asks for this on connect and after every selection
 // change, so it has to fail quickly rather than hold up a configuration reply.
+//
+// It is per request, not for both together. Until 20 Nov 2026 one deadline covered the manifest and
+// its signature, so a slow manifest left the signature whatever was left over — and the failure read
+// "the manifest has no signature", which points at the release rather than at the clock running out.
+//
+// Measured on a Dot over Wi-Fi, eight rounds of both fetches: 2, 9, 2, 5, 9, 16, 6, 5 seconds in
+// total. Every single request succeeded; three rounds simply did not fit in ten seconds between
+// them. Two independent deadlines leave the common case exactly as it was and only lengthen the
+// worst case, which is the one already failing.
 const manifestTimeout = 10 * time.Second
 
